A rolling deploy sends SIGKILL to the pod right after the transaction committed, before the first publish attempt.

Statement: "The enrollment row stays committed and its enrollment.created event is never published; the retry loop cannot help, because it only exists in the process that was killed."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The commit is durable, so the row survives the kill. The fact that an event still has to be sent lives only in the memory of the killed process: the loop, its attempt counter and the enrollment object disappear with it, and nothing after a restart knows about this enrollment. Retries only help with failures the process survives, and even then only for outages shorter than about 6 seconds here. The common misconception is that "retry with backoff" makes the publish reliable; it makes it more likely, not guaranteed. A durable record of the pending event (an outbox row in the same commit) is what survives the kill.', 1, 5
