10:14:02.240 saga=s_7f2 compensate=refund           ok  wallet u_381: 200 -> 1200
10:14:04.903 saga=s_7f2 compensate=cancelEnrollment ok  enrollment e_9902 cancelled

progress-service 10:14:03.518 lesson_started enrollment=e_9902 lesson=1

Statement: "Running refund before cancelEnrollment left about 2.6 seconds in which learner u_381 had an active enrollment in a premium course that was no longer paid for, and the progress service accepted a lesson in that window."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Between 10:14:02.240 (points returned) and 10:14:04.903 (enrollment cancelled) the database held an active enrollment with a refunded payment, a state the forward flow can never produce, and the progress service used it at 10:14:03.518. Compensating in reverse order (cancelEnrollment, then refund) keeps every intermediate state equal to some prefix of the forward flow: first "paid, not enrolled", then "nothing". The misconception is that the order of compensations does not matter because the end state is the same: other services read the intermediate states too, because a saga has no isolation.', 1, 5
