WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dce_411'), 'The refund compensation of the points wallet:

async function refund(sagaId: string) {
  const p = await db.one("SELECT user_id, amount FROM payments WHERE saga_id = $1", [sagaId]);
  await db.none("UPDATE wallets SET balance = balance + $1 WHERE user_id = $2", [p.amount, p.user_id]);
  await db.none("UPDATE payments SET status = ''refunded'' WHERE saga_id = $1", [sagaId]);
}

Orchestrator log:
11:02:40.004 saga=s_a11 compensate=refund attempt=1 error=TimeoutError after 5000 ms
11:02:41.012 saga=s_a11 compensate=refund attempt=2 ok

Statement: "The retry is safe: attempt 1 ended with an error, so its UPDATE statements did not change the wallet, and attempt 2 credited the points exactly once."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. A timeout tells the orchestrator only that it stopped waiting; the database may have committed attempt 1 a moment later. This refund also never checks the payment status, and its two UPDATEs are not even in one transaction, so attempt 2 adds the points again whenever attempt 1 got as far as the wallet update. A compensation must be idempotent: in one transaction, update only a payment that is still ''captured'' (UPDATE payments SET status = ''refunded'' WHERE saga_id = $1 AND status = ''captured'' RETURNING amount) and credit the wallet only when that returned a row. The misconception is that an error from a call means the call had no effect.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dce_411') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
