WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dce_431'), 'During the spike, the certificate service consumes the CDC topic of the enrollments table directly. A change event (simplified):

{ "op": "u",
  "before": { "id": "e_9902", "status": "active", "completed_at": null, "internal_note": null },
  "after":  { "id": "e_9902", "status": "completed", "completed_at": "2026-10-22T09:14:03Z", "internal_note": null },
  "source": { "table": "enrollments", "lsn": 81234560 } }

Consumer code:
if (msg.after?.status === "completed" && msg.before?.status !== "completed") issueCertificate(msg.after.id);

The enrollment team plans this migration for next week, with no change to any event contract they publish:
ALTER TABLE enrollments RENAME COLUMN status TO state;

Statement: "After this migration the certificate service stops issuing certificates, even though the enrollment team changed no event they own."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. With CDC on a business table, the table''s columns are the message format. After the rename, after.status is undefined, the condition is never true, and no error is raised: certificates silently stop. The enrollment team treats the table as private and has no idea it became a public contract. A transactional outbox avoids this by publishing an explicit event (EnrollmentCompleted with its own fields and version) that the team maintains on purpose, and CDC on the outbox table keeps that benefit. The misconception is that CDC is free of coupling because the application code does not change; the coupling moves to the schema.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dce_431') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
