WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dce_511'), 'The producer added one optional field, completedLessons, to ProgressRecorded v2. Nothing else changed. The leaderboard consumer validates with zod:

const ProgressV2 = z.object({
  enrollmentId: z.string(),
  learnerId: z.string(),
  courseId: z.string(),
  percent: z.number().min(0).max(100),
}).strict();

leaderboard-consumer 09:41:07 WARN validation failed, sending to DLQ
  eventId=evt_a204 issues=[{"code":"unrecognized_keys","keys":["completedLessons"]}]

Statement: "Removing .strict() from this schema lets the consumer process the new events, while an event that has no courseId is still rejected."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. .strict() makes zod reject any key the schema does not declare, so a purely additive producer change sends every event to the DLQ. Without it, z.object strips unknown keys by default and still enforces the declared ones: courseId stays required, so an event without it still fails validation. That is a tolerant reader: strict about the fields this consumer uses, indifferent to fields it does not know. The misconception is that tolerance means accepting anything; a tolerant reader still rejects events that lack what it needs.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dce_511') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
