    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dce_521'), 'Saturday 10:30. Progress events are keyed by enrollmentId.

$ kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server kafka:9092 --describe --group leaderboard
GROUP        TOPIC              PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG
leaderboard  progress.recorded  0          1204551         1204553         2
leaderboard  progress.recorded  1          1198032         1198032         0
leaderboard  progress.recorded  2          1187340         1229222         41882
leaderboard  progress.recorded  3          1201877         1201880         3

Alert rule (not firing, current value 10471.75):
  expr: avg by (group) (kafka_consumergroup_lag) > 20000
  for: 10m

Statement: "Because this alert is not firing, no learner is waiting longer than usual for a leaderboard update."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The average divides one stuck partition by four. Partition 2 is 41,882 messages behind while the others are at zero or near it, and since events are keyed by enrollmentId, every learner whose enrollment hashes to partition 2 is waiting, about a quarter of all learners. Alert on the worst partition (max by group) and preferably on time lag, which means the same thing at peak and at night. The misconception is that an aggregate over partitions describes each learner''s experience; ordering per key makes delays per partition, not per group.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dce_521') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
